{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs at merge commit 126c940817a99b4efafc19d8f34b41750800e064. limits.md documents the shipped sequencer queue bound and the boundary of what the back-pressure sentinel promises.\n\nWHAT MAIN NOW CARRIES. A Concurrent submissions section: the resident admits 32 submissions at once counting the lock holder, and refuses beyond that with \"sequencer at capacity\", taken before parsing, admission hooks, payload-tree writes and chaining. A zero limit is the embedding opt-out. And the paragraph that keeps the claim honest: ErrBackPressure also reports an exhausted retry limit after contention, so only the capacity refusal is free — a caller must not read \"leaves no object behind\" as a guarantee about every ErrBackPressure. The page cites 45f88594 at internal/kernel and 0ea5a63a at internal/app.\n\nThat qualifying paragraph is the part I would point a reader at. The draft I inherited described only the cheap case and stated it over the sentinel as a whole, which is false in the direction that matters: someone trusting it would assume a failed submission left the repository untouched. Checking the claim against kernel.go rather than carrying the sentence forward is what found it.\n\nSUCCESSION AT THIS PATH IS COMPLETE. Two live artifacts covered it, b930725a at 27603774 and the candidate 63f63d9e at acf13441, both mine, and neither cited by any page — I checked before retiring rather than after. Both are retired against this record, so docs carries exactly one live artifact.\n\nThat is not true of the two code paths in this same merge, and the difference is worth naming. internal/kernel and internal/app keep several live predecessors because pages cite them — d97eed89 by eight pages, 82a33e46 by two, and the two candidates by limits.md. Retiring a cited artifact is fatal to the citation gate, so those retirements have to travel with the re-anchoring of the citing pages, in one head, under their own request. Here nothing cited the predecessors, s